00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:54.850
Nostoc are a genus of cyanobacteria known
for gathering their thin, thread-like bodies

00:00:54.850 --> 00:01:01.510
into gelatinous colonies, forming what look
like, basically, a weird noodle soup.

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:07.800
Of course you might not want your bowl of noodle
soup to be bright green like this, and that is fair.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:13.120
But outside the world of imagined meals, the
color of cyanobacteria like nostoc are an

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:19.510
indicator of their life-sustaining ability,
turning their chlorophyll into a vivid display

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:23.570
as they reflect green wavelengths into our
vision.

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:32.150
Then, with a flick of a switch, we can change
the light on our sample from white light to ultraviolet light.

00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:34.880
And everything changes.

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:43.060
Our soup of filaments is now a bright, bright
red, like we just doused our noodles in hot sauce.

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:49.670
To be clear, there were no filters involved
in changing the color of the chlorophyll molecule.

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:54.360
And we did not add any kind of chemical to
make the nostoc glow red.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:57.350
The nostoc is making that light.

00:01:57.350 --> 00:02:03.640
All we did was flip a switch, and electrons
did the rest.

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:06.880
What the nostoc are doing here is fluorescing.

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:11.189
And as you will see today, they are not the
only organisms who can do this.

00:02:11.189 --> 00:02:14.930
Throughout this video, we’re going to be
showing you different organisms, first under

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:19.719
white light, and then under one of the more
narrow bands of light that are part of our

00:02:19.719 --> 00:02:22.909
new fluorescence microscopy toolkit.

00:02:22.909 --> 00:02:29.329
Fluorescence is what it looks like when light
makes electrons jump around in their orbitals.

00:02:29.329 --> 00:02:35.900
Inside an organism there are many, many chemicals,
and inside those chemicals are atoms and bonds

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:41.590
and electrons arranged in a way that makes
life possible.

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:46.310
Now depending on the structure of those atoms
and bonds, sometimes certain wavelengths of

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:52.200
light will excite the electrons, sending them
jumping up to a higher energy state.

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:56.669
But those electrons will very quickly fall
back down to their original state.

00:02:56.669 --> 00:03:02.030
And when they do that, those electrons release
photons.

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:07.099
And that light is what we’re seeing here,
the emission light--bright against a dark

00:03:07.099 --> 00:03:12.159
backdrop, while a filter blocks the original
excitation from obscuring the sight of our

00:03:12.159 --> 00:03:13.920
organism fluorescing.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:18.709
When a chemical is able to produce fluorescence,
it’s called a fluorophore.

00:03:18.709 --> 00:03:23.700
And for our nostoc and other photosynthetic
organisms, we know that the fluorophore glowing

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:26.430
red under UV light is chlorophyll.

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:33.659
But we know this only because we know enough
about the cyanobacteria’s biology and chlorophyll’s

00:03:33.659 --> 00:03:35.640
fluorescence to narrow down the source.

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:40.829
For other organisms, the source of the fluorescence
is a mystery to us, though with the right

00:03:40.829 --> 00:03:45.019
combination of tools and experiments, we might
be able to narrow down the source4.

00:03:45.019 --> 00:03:49.231
When we see this fluorescence, we know something
exciting is happening in our organism, we

00:03:49.231 --> 00:03:51.939
just don’t know exactly where it’s coming
from.

00:03:51.939 --> 00:03:54.469
The only clue we have is the color orange.

00:03:54.469 --> 00:03:58.980
And since there are all sorts of possible
fluorophores in the world, we don’t even

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:03.040
know if there’s even just one molecule making
this light.

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:07.999
We might be watching several fluorophores
at work here, their excited electrons lighting

00:04:07.999 --> 00:04:10.209
up the organism in unison.

00:04:10.209 --> 00:04:16.739
Now fluorescence is when organisms take in
light and then reemit it a different wavelength.

00:04:16.739 --> 00:04:19.329
But some organisms can make their own light.

00:04:19.329 --> 00:04:23.930
And perhaps the most famous glowing residents
of the microcosmos are the dinoflagellates,

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:28.330
whose bioluminescent light you can sometimes
see across the surface of waves.

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:30.650
That light is not from fluorescence.

00:04:30.650 --> 00:04:36.289
It is the result of a molecule called luciferin,
which emits light when it’s oxidized by

00:04:36.289 --> 00:04:38.240
the luciferin enzyme.

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:41.870
This is a chemical process rather than a physical
one.

00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:47.889
The effect is similar to fluorescence: a glimmer
of incandescent light coming from within a microscopic body.

00:04:47.889 --> 00:04:50.509
But the cause is entirely different.

00:04:50.509 --> 00:04:57.080
Dinoflagellates are bioluminescent, their
light is the result of chemicals reacting with one another.

00:04:57.080 --> 00:05:02.960
Chlorophyll and other fluorophores, however,
don’t require another chemical to light up.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:05.400
They just need the right wavelength of light
to hit them.

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:08.680
They then use that light to make light.

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:14.770
But while bioluminescence and fluorescence
are different phenomena, biology loves to

00:05:14.770 --> 00:05:19.870
muddy the boundaries between the categories
we assign to things, and there are moments

00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:25.419
where these two seemingly distinct phenomena
are in fact linked together.

00:05:25.419 --> 00:05:29.850
Off the west coast of North America, there’s
a bioluminescent jellyfish called Aequorea

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:36.030
victoria, which shines blue when calcium activates
a molecule called aequorin.

00:05:36.030 --> 00:05:41.560
But the light from that reaction is able to
provide energy to another protein, a protein

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:48.830
that fluoresces green when hit by that light
and it gives the jellyfish a somewhat more verdant hue.

00:05:48.830 --> 00:05:56.319
When scientists found this protein, they gave
it the very literal name of Green Fluorescent Protein, or GFP.

00:05:56.319 --> 00:06:00.639
But there’s nothing wrong with a name that
cuts right to the chase like that, especially

00:06:00.639 --> 00:06:05.689
when you’re naming what has since become
one of the most famous fluorescent proteins.

00:06:05.689 --> 00:06:12.580
The beauty of fluorescent proteins like GFP
goes beyond just the colors they show us under the microscope.

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:20.340
They have allowed scientists new ways to illuminate
organisms from the inside, to introduce fluorescent

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:28.200
proteins through stains or genetic engineering
into an organism like a fluorescent molecular marker.

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:33.520
And from there, the fluorescence helps us
map out the events inside a cell that might

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:40.159
otherwise be invisible, like the dynamics
of gene expression or the course of a cell’s fate.

00:06:40.159 --> 00:06:45.210
Often, when you see those stunning images
of cells awash in a rainbow haze, you’re

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:50.009
looking at the careful work of decades of
science, of the lessons we’ve learned from

00:06:50.009 --> 00:06:57.710
nature about how to see it in a new way.

00:06:57.710 --> 00:07:02.900
And as we apply these lessons, it’s important
for us to make sure we give the organism’s

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:08.740
their due for the fluorescence made of their
own structures--like the red glow of the Nostoc’s

00:07:08.740 --> 00:07:10.800
chlorophyll, which we call autofluorescence.

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:14.169
But it’s not simply a matter of giving credit.

00:07:14.169 --> 00:07:19.980
In one form, autofluorescence illuminates,
providing us with another way to see the molecules

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:21.830
of an organism at work.

00:07:21.830 --> 00:07:26.729
The autofluorescence of chlorophyll can, for
example, tell us more about the health of a plant.

00:07:26.729 --> 00:07:31.010
Yet in another form, autofluorescence obscures.

00:07:31.010 --> 00:07:35.289
If we introduce our own fluorescent markers
into a cell, only to find that the cell’s

00:07:35.289 --> 00:07:42.099
own molecules produce a light similar to our
markers’, the colors become very difficult to interpret.

00:07:42.099 --> 00:07:47.080
And so the usefulness of autofluorescence
ultimately becomes decided by its context.

00:07:47.080 --> 00:07:52.409
For the scientist, it is a consideration as
experiments are planned and interpreted.

00:07:52.409 --> 00:07:58.900
For the organism, it is simply a reaction
in its own body: a light made into new light,

00:07:58.900 --> 00:08:01.819
a color made into new color.

00:08:01.819 --> 00:08:10.539
And for us, it is a glimmer of something new,
made by old friends.

